    "body": "HTML Constructor step 8.2, like step 11, also does \"Perform element.[\\[SetPrototypeOf]\\](prototype). Rethrow any exceptions.\" (Incidentally, I don't think this *can* cause exceptions?)\r\n\r\nIn practice as long as ES object creation remains unobservable it probably doesn't matter whether that stack is Element or ES object.\r\n\r\nMy $0.02: the construction stack should contain Elements (in the sense of IDL type) and not ES objects.\r\n\r\nIn practice our implementation (Blink) puts pointers to native Element instances on that stack, which we can wrap at any time to get an ES object. This feels right because Element is more specific (we can wrap one under most circumstances) than ES object (not all ES objects are wrappers, and not all wrappers wrap Elements.) Also the act of wrapper creation is better the closer it is to the constructor which needs the wrapper; for example, in future if someone specified how custom elements in extensions worked. Our implementation can tolerate custom element-like things that are defined in C++, not JavaScript, and we use this in unit tests; if a non-ECMAScript WebIDL binding ever came back then having that stack be Element and not an ES object would be better.\r\n\r\nFWIW all the implementations I can find use native pointers in practice: WebKit (native Element&; [here](https://github.com/WebKit/webkit/blob/0eace3ea527df44d99e8cb5e7c2bfecc63392d82/Source/WebCore/bindings/js/JSCustomElementInterface.cpp#L200)); Gecko (nsGenericHTMLElement; [here](https://hg.mozilla.org/mozilla-central/file/a73cc4e08bf5/dom/base/CustomElementRegistry.cpp#l993)) and Blink (Member&lt;Element&gt; [here](https://cs.chromium.org/chromium/src/third_party/WebKit/Source/core/html/custom/CustomElementDefinition.cpp?sq=package:chromium&l=124)).",
